 <h3 id="tunnel">The Node64 tunnel</h3>
-<p>In modes 1 and 2, Node64 opens an outgoing tunnel to each edge server (QUIC over UDP, port 7844 or 443; if UDP is blocked, TLS over TCP). For your router this is a normal outgoing connection. Each tunnel logs in with its own token. Technical details – ports, fallback to TCP, keepalive – are explained in the Node64 docs under <a href="https://node64.de/en/docs/cdn64-online/#wer-verbindet-sich" rel="noopener">CDN64 – tunnel without port forwarding</a>.</p>
+<p>In modes 1 and 2, Node64 opens an outgoing tunnel to each edge server of your account (QUIC over UDP, port 7844 or 443; if UDP is blocked, TLS over TCP). For your router this is a normal outgoing connection. Each tunnel logs in with its own token. Technical details – ports, fallback to TCP, keepalive – are explained in the Node64 docs under <a href="https://node64.de/en/docs/cdn64-online/#wer-verbindet-sich" rel="noopener">CDN64 – tunnel without port forwarding</a>.</p>
 <h3 id="lastverteilung">Load balancing and failover</h3>
 <ul>
-	<li><strong>Several servers via DNS:</strong> DNS returns the addresses of all edge servers, so visitors are spread across Nuremberg and Falkenstein automatically.</li>
-	<li><strong>A server fails:</strong> if an edge server does not report to IPv64.net for 45 seconds and does not answer a direct check either, IPv64.net removes its addresses from the DNS of all CDN64 names – as long as another server is reachable. Once it is back for 30 seconds, its addresses return.</li>
+	<li><strong>Several servers via DNS:</strong> DNS returns the addresses of your account's edge servers. From two servers on, one is in Nuremberg and one in Falkenstein; visitors are spread across them automatically.</li>
+	<li><strong>A server fails:</strong> if an edge server does not report to IPv64.net for 45 seconds and does not answer a direct check either, IPv64.net removes its addresses from DNS – as long as another server is reachable. Until then the other servers of your account carry the load. If it stays away for more than 3 minutes (so not just a restart), another server stands in for the accounts it is assigned to, preferably at the same location: DNS gets that server's addresses, and Node64 additionally connects to it within a minute – without interrupting the connections to the other servers. If your account has only one server (Free), the name is unreachable until then. Once the server is back for 30 seconds, the previous assignment applies again.</li>
 	<li><strong>Parallel connections:</strong> depending on your account class, Node64 opens several parallel tunnel connections to each server (Business: 2, Company: 4, see <a href="#kontingent">quota</a>). The servers hand new visitors to the least loaded connection.</li>
 	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> a name can be assigned to several tunnels (i.e. several Node64 instances). The order is the priority: the first tunnel that is currently connected serves the name. This is a fallback, not load balancing between nodes. You set the list via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#name-aendern">API</a> (field <code>tunnels</code>).</li>
 </ul>
+<h3 id="server-je-konto">Servers per account</h3>
+<p>Not every account uses all edge servers. Your account class decides how many: Free 1, Home / Supporter 2, Business 1 or 2 (your choice), Company 1 to all (your choice; “all” includes future servers). The number applies to all names of the account and to all modes, including mode 3.</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> IPv64.net assigns the servers automatically – one per location first, each time the one with the fewest accounts. The assignment stays fixed until the number changes. ipv64.net/cdn64 shows the servers your account currently uses in the <em>Servers</em> line.</li>
+	<li><strong>Choosing the number (Business, Company):</strong> on ipv64.net/cdn64 in the <em>Servers</em> line or via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#server">API</a>. IPv64.net then updates the DNS of all names; Node64 opens its tunnels to the new servers within a minute.</li>
+	<li><strong>Class changed:</strong> the new number applies within a few minutes; servers that remain are the ones assigned first.</li>
+	<li><strong>Old DNS answer at the visitor:</strong> if a visitor still reaches a server your account no longer uses, that server passes them on to a server with your tunnel.</li>
+</ul>
 
 <h2 id="modi">The three modes</h2>
 <p>The mode decides where encryption (TLS) ends and how the edge servers reach your target.</p>
